index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..c1cb8c5b7dfd17945a2dbedc9dbaf0db8b9f32dc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/task/task-002-implement-rfc-6902-json-patch-applypatch.md
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-rfc-6902-json-patch-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch applyPatch"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with its six operations: add, remove, replace, move, copy and test.
+Export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a patch (an array of operations)
+to a JSON document and returns the result as a new value, without mutating the input. It throws when
+the patch is invalid or when one of its operations fails.
+
+## Work done
+
+- `test/apply-patch.test.ts`: 41 tests, written first. Before the implementation existed the file failed
+  on the missing `applyPatch` export. They cover each operation's happy path and error path (RFC 6902
+  sections 4.1 to 4.6), the rule that `move` cannot move a location into its own child, array insertion
+  and `-`, input immutability, independence of the result from patch values, a `__proto__` member name,
+  and patch validation.
+- `src/index.ts`: `applyPatch`, plus the exported types `JsonPatchOperation` and `JsonPatch`. It reuses
+  the RFC 6901 `parsePointer` and `resolvePointer`, so both standards share one definition of a valid
+  pointer.
+- Results: `npm test` reports 54 pass, 0 fail (13 pointer tests and 41 patch tests). `tsc --noEmit` with
+  the project's strict `tsconfig.json` is clean.
+- Design choices are recorded in dl-002 (copy, not mutate), dl-003 (validate first, all or nothing) and
+  dl-004 (clone values; set members as data).
+
+## Open points
+
+- Removing the whole document (`path` `""`) throws: the result would have no `JsonValue`.
+- The `value` of an operation is checked for presence only, not as deep JSON. The document is typed
+  `JsonValue` and no runtime check of the document was added, consistent with `resolvePointer`.
